# Brewfile — the shell host's toolchain, declared (superproject #421).
#
# Homebrew on Linux ("Linuxbrew") installs to /home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew and runs as the
# login user, never root. `brew bundle` makes it idempotent and declarative, which is why
# brew earns a place on a converge-managed box at all: this file is the desired state, not
# a list of things somebody once typed.
#
# ⚠ CASKS DO NOT WORK ON LINUX. Homebrew casks are macOS-only, so anything that only ships
#   as a cask cannot come from here — Claude Code is exactly that case (it is a cask, 404 as
#   a formula), and is installed from Anthropic's signed apt repo instead.
#
# ⚠ tmux IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and adding it would break your session.
#   tmux's client and server must be the SAME VERSION. The server on this host is started by
#   the systemd unit as /usr/bin/tmux (Debian's 3.5a). If brew's tmux were installed, brew's
#   bin directory comes FIRST on PATH, so an interactive `tmux attach` would run a different
#   binary than the running server and fail with "protocol version mismatch" — breaking the
#   one thing this host exists to provide. Debian's tmux is current; leave it alone.
#   A test in the superproject asserts this file contains no tmux entry.

# .NET — what we build with day to day. The formula ships an x86_64_linux bottle at the same
# version the laptop runs, and satisfies the repo's global.json (10.0.100, rollForward
# latestMinor), so `dotnet build` behaves the same here as locally.
brew "dotnet"

# Git forge CLI — PRs and issues from the session, same as on the laptop.
brew "gh"

# The search kit Claude Code and day-to-day work both lean on. ripgrep is bundled inside
# Claude Code, but having it on PATH is useful for everything else.
brew "ripgrep"
brew "fd"
brew "jq"

# Quality-of-life for living in a terminal for long stretches.
brew "bat"
brew "eza"
brew "htop"
brew "tree"

# ── Zellij — a second multiplexer, running SIDE BY SIDE with tmux, not replacing it.
#
# On trial as the answer to a specific problem rather than as a taste preference: it ships
# a BUILT-IN WEB CLIENT (`zellij web`, 0.43+), which is a browser terminal served by the
# host itself. If that earns its keep it lets the public surface for this box be an ordinary
# HTTP resource instead of Pangolin's `mode: ssh` resource — and, more to the point, lets
# SSH here go back to keys only, since the browser route no longer needs an OS PASSWORD on
# the account (superproject #440 / SHELL_USER_PASSWORD). Session resurrection is native, so
# it also subsumes what tmux-resurrect/continuum do via TPM.
#
# tmux stays the default and keeps its curated config. Nothing here switches over; this
# installs the binary so both can be lived in and compared.
#
# ⚠ NONE of tmux's dual-source hazard applies, and it is worth being explicit about why,
#   because the rule above says brew must not supply a multiplexer. tmux's problem is TWO
#   copies: apt's at /usr/bin (what the systemd unit starts) and brew's earlier on PATH, so
#   client and server disagree on protocol version. zellij is NOT PACKAGED IN DEBIAN AT ALL
#   (verified: no `zellij` in trixie's apt index), so brew is the only source and there is no
#   second binary to disagree with. It is also why it has to come from here — 0.45 vs
#   whatever a backport might one day carry, and the web client only exists from 0.43.
#
# ⚠ The web server REFUSES TO START without a config file. `zellij` itself runs fine on
#   built-in defaults, but `zellij web` logs "Failed to find default config file path" and
#   exits within a second, having already printed "Web Server started" — so it looks like it
#   came up. It needs ~/.config/zellij/config.kdl to exist (`zellij setup --dump-config`).
#   It also refuses any non-loopback bind without a cert: "Cannot bind to non-loopback IP:
#   0.0.0.0 without an SSL certificate."
brew "zellij"

# ── PowerShell. Not a preference — this repo family runs on it. Every script in the
# superproject's src/Proxmox/ ships a .ps1 twin that must stay functionally in sync with its
# .sh, secrets-sync has a .ps1 variant, and the house rule for .NET repos is that build/codegen
# tooling is a Fallout target or PowerShell rather than bash or python. Without pwsh here, half
# of what this box exists to work on cannot be run, only read.
brew "powershell"

# ── Node. Needed less for JS projects than for the ecosystem an agent reaches into: most MCP
# servers are npx-launched, and a lot of dev tooling assumes a node runtime is simply present.
# Claude Code itself does NOT need this — it ships its own runtime — so this is for everything
# else, and it is the least load-bearing entry here.
brew "node"

# ── Bitwarden CLI (`bw`) — the password vault half of the secrets story. CLAUDE.md's rule is
# that generated credentials go to Bitwarden, and doing that from this box needs the client.
#
# ⚠ This is NOT `bws`. The Secrets Manager CLI that `scripts/secrets-sync.sh` calls to generate
#   secrets.env is a different tool, is not in homebrew-core under any name (checked: `bws` and
#   `bitwarden-secrets-manager` both miss), and would have to be fetched from its GitHub release
#   the way NewtProvisioner fetches newt. Until then this box cannot regenerate secrets.env.
brew "bitwarden-cli"
